  "slug": "structure-fast-6329",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a Briefings in Bioinformatics paper target compact, context-specific gene sets and priors for regulatory inference.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is conditioning the inputs to network inference, such as gene sets, priors, or perturbation context, so that the inferred regulatory structure reflects a specific biological setting. GSET selects compact gene sets from temporal gene embeddings using optimal transport residuals and KL divergence for mechanistic GRN inference. MAGE searches for maximal and minimal Age Index gene sets within time-varying gene regulatory networks. REGA-perturb links genetic perturbations to TF-regulatory element-gene paths using reference epigenomes and pooled latent programs from Perturb-seq. On the phosphoproteomic side, a benchmark of Kinase Library-expanded kinase-substrate priors across 222 samples reports that protein-association filtering improves kinase activity inference. What is new is the emphasis on contextual refinement of priors and gene sets rather than on a single inference model. The items differ in modality and maturity, so this describes a dominant thread rather than a shared method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
